1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 9, The Map of Positions.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,320
In 1978, the philosopher Ned Block asked you to imagine the following.

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:13,120
Take the population of China, more than a billion people.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:22,040
Give each person a two-way radio and one simple rule to follow, so that together they implement the functional organization that describes your mind.

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:26,740
Wire the whole network up to an artificial body and run it for an hour.

6
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:34,400
If the right functional organization is all there is to having a mind, then during that hour, the nation of China should feel what you feel.

7
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:38,820
If you would be in pain, then something, somewhere, should be in pain.

8
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:45,040
Block found that very hard to believe and argued that the burden of proof falls on the functionalist.

9
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,180
Many people share the intuition.

10
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:55,460
My intuition says no, but my intuition also says that a lump of wet meat should not be able to think, and here we are.

11
00:00:56,740 --> 00:01:02,380
science fiction story, they're made out of meat, in which aliens refuse to believe that humans

12
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:08,300
think with meat. I'm not sure our intuitions about substrates are worth much. That's exactly

13
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:13,760
the right worry to carry through this episode. Every position on this map is partly an answer

14
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:19,560
to the question, which intuitions should we trust? Two episodes ago, Chalmers' six letters

15
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,940
sorted the responses to the hard problem, and last episode gave the most radical one a full hearing.

16
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:32,380
of the territory. For each position, the core claim, the best argument, the strongest objection,

17
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:37,040
and its verdict on the question this series builds toward. Could a machine be conscious?

18
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:41,260
At the end, a short, clearly marked trip to the edges of the map.

19
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,840
Start with functionalism, because it's the default view among philosophers who work on mind

20
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:53,760
and the implicit view of most people who build AI. Functionalism grew out of a problem with an

21
00:01:53,760 --> 00:02:00,160
earlier theory. In the 1950s, Ullen Place and JJC Smart proposed the identity theory.

22
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:08,000
Mental states simply are brain states. Pain is a certain neural process, full stop. Then in 1967,

23
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:13,640
Hilary Putnam pointed out that this was too narrow. An octopus can presumably feel pain,

24
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:19,520
but its nervous system evolved independently and is built very differently. So if pain is

25
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:25,740
one specific human brain state, the octopus cannot have it. Putnam's conclusion was multiple

26
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:31,300
realizability. The same mental state can be realized by very different physical systems.

27
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:36,580
So mental states should be defined by their function, by their causal role, rather than by

28
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:42,900
their physical makeup. Pain is whatever state is caused by bodily damage, and in turn causes

29
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:48,700
distress, avoidance, and the desire for it to stop. That's the octopus from episode two,

30
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:55,040
now doing philosophical work. Complex minds evolving more than once on completely different

31
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:59,940
hardware is one of the strongest everyday reasons to find functionalism plausible.

32
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:06,440
Computational functionalism is the stronger version. The relevant causal roles are computational.

33
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,680
Implement the right computation, in neurons or silicon,

34
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:13,600
and you get the mind, experience included.

35
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:16,840
The hardest question for functionalism is one of grain.

36
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:19,120
Which functional organization matters?

37
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:23,160
At one extreme, you could define it purely by inputs and outputs.

38
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:28,600
Block demolished that version in 1981 with a thought experiment now called Blockhead,

39
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:31,640
a machine that passes any conversational test

40
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:35,520
by looking up every possible reply in an astronomically large table

41
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:42,840
advance. Behaviorally perfect, intuitively mindless. In fairness, that table would need

42
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:48,580
vastly more entries than there are particles in the universe. And whatever large language models

43
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:54,680
are, they are not look-up tables. They compress. That's the whole point of the training process.

44
00:03:55,180 --> 00:04:00,200
Agreed and Block conceded the machine was physically impossible. His point was that

45
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:05,440
behavior alone cannot settle the question, so a functionalist must say which internal organization

46
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:09,100
Too coarse, and you count blockhead.

47
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:14,460
Too fine, down to individual neurons, and you exclude octopuses and machines.

48
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,860
Whereinbetween turns out to matter enormously for AI?

49
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:24,580
The best argument for functionalism about experience, not just thought, comes surprisingly from a dualist.

50
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:30,700
Chalmers' fading qualia argument asks you to imagine your neurons being replaced one at a time

51
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:35,480
by silicon chips that perfectly duplicate each neuron's input-output behavior.

52
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:42,960
behavior never changes and you keep saying, I see a vivid red. If your experience gradually faded as

53
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:49,120
the chips went in, then at some point you'd be having faded or absent experiences while sincerely

54
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:55,120
reporting vivid ones, systematically wrong about your own mind with no internal sign of it. A

55
00:04:55,120 --> 00:05:00,380
companion argument, dancing qualia, imagines flipping between your neurons and their silicon

56
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:06,120
twins with a switch. If experience depended on the substrate, your red could turn blue and back

57
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:12,740
while you noticed nothing. Chalmers finds both scenarios implausible and concludes that experience

58
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:17,780
depends only on fine-grained functional organization. He calls this the principle

59
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:23,880
of organizational invariance. The objections are mostly intuition pumps, the China brain,

60
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:28,360
the absent qualia worry that a functional duplicate might have no experience at all,

61
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:33,220
and the inverted spectrum, the idea that someone could be functionally identical to you

62
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:38,560
while seeing red where you see green. Functionalism's verdict on machines. Yes,

63
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:44,200
a machine can be conscious if it has the right organization. The fight is over what right means.

64
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:50,700
The second major physicalist family is representationalism, sometimes called

65
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:56,740
intentionalism. The idea is that the character of an experience is fully determined by what it

66
00:05:56,740 --> 00:06:02,220
represents. When you see a red tomato, the redness is not a property of your experience.

67
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:08,380
property your experience represents the tomato as having. The main motivation is called the

68
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:14,460
transparency of experience, a point Gilbert Harmon made vivid. Look at a tree and try to attend to

69
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:20,060
your experience of it rather than to the tree. Most people find they cannot. Every feature they

70
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:26,120
notice, the green, the shape, the depth, is presented as a feature of the tree. Introspection

71
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:31,700
seems to look straight through experience to the world. Michael Tai and Fred Dretzk built the best

72
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:37,880
Classic objections involve cases where experience and representation seem to come apart.

73
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:42,060
One is blurry vision, where the world is not represented as blurry.

74
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:46,380
Another is Bloch's inverted earth, a planet where colors are swapped.

75
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,560
A traveler there, wearing color-inverting lenses,

76
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:55,940
eventually comes to represent colors correctly while, intuitively, their experience stays the same.

77
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:59,400
Here's why representationalism matters for AI.

78
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:03,880
On one leading family of theories of what makes something a representation,

79
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:09,060
called teleosmantic theories, a state represents what it was selected to track,

80
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:11,560
typically by evolution or learning.

81
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,600
Donald Davidson's Swampman makes the problem vivid.

82
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:20,860
Lightning strikes a swamp and by sheer chance assembles a molecule-for-molecule duplicative view.

83
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:25,640
On a strict teleosmantic view, Swampman states represent nothing,

84
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:30,420
having no history of selection, so Swampman would have no experiences at all.

85
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:35,200
Then the question for a language model is whether training counts as a history.

86
00:07:35,780 --> 00:07:40,800
Gradient descent is a selection process. It shaped every weight to track something.

87
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,980
That's exactly what some philosophers have argued.

88
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:50,300
Dimitri Quelyou, Malo, and Raphael Miliere, for instance, have argued that language models can

89
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:55,780
have genuinely grounded representations, particularly once fine-tuning with human feedback,

90
00:07:56,120 --> 00:08:02,060
gives their outputs world-directed functions. So representationalism's verdict on machines is,

91
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:07,640
possibly, depending on whether the system's representations are genuinely about the world,

92
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:12,360
and on the further question of which representations count as experiential.

93
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:19,520
Last episode's position, illusionism, belongs here on the map as the strongest form of type

94
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:23,300
Its verdict on machines, in one sentence,

95
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,720
a machine could be conscious in the only sense there is

96
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:29,200
if it has the relevant kind of self-model,

97
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:33,540
producing introspective representations of its own states for the right reasons,

98
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:36,180
and not merely because it learned our words.

99
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:41,560
Now a family that comes at the problem from biology rather than computation,

100
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:42,700
an activism.

101
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:49,900
In 1991, Francisco Varela, Evan Thompson, and Alina Roche published The Embodied Mind,

102
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:54,260
arguing that cognition is not the manipulation of internal representations,

103
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:59,120
but the activity of a living organism enacting a world through its body.

104
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:05,080
It builds on autopoiesis, the self-producing organization we met in episode 4.

105
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:09,440
Thompson's Mind and Life argues for a deep continuity between life and mind.

106
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:16,760
In 2001, Kevin Oregon and Alva Noai proposed, in a target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

107
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,240
that seeing is a kind of skilled activity.

108
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:25,060
of the ways sensory input changes as you move. And Naya puts the slogan bluntly,

109
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:31,240
consciousness is something we do, not something that happens inside us. Varela also proposed

110
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:36,460
neurophenomenology, pairing rigorous, trained first-person reports, including from experienced

111
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:41,120
meditators, with brain measurements so that experience and physiology constrain each other.

112
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:46,080
That program comes back in the next episode. An activism's verdict on machines,

113
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:50,520
Consciousness belongs to autonomous, self-producing, embodied systems.

114
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,560
Today's disembodied models are poor candidates.

115
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:57,140
Artificial lifelike systems someday might not be.

116
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:00,760
Next, biological naturalism.

117
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:08,680
John Searle, who died in 2025, published The Chinese Room in 1980 as a target article with commentaries,

118
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:13,520
and it became perhaps the most discussed thought experiment in the philosophy of AI.

119
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:19,060
Searle imagined himself locked in a room with a rulebook for manipulating Chinese characters.

120
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:22,300
Questions in Chinese come in through a slot.

121
00:10:22,460 --> 00:10:27,120
By following the rules, he sends out answers indistinguishable from a native speakers.

122
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:29,680
But Searle understands no Chinese.

123
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:33,500
Since a computer running a program does nothing more than he does,

124
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:35,900
following rules for manipulating symbols,

125
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:40,380
he concluded that running a program can never by itself produce understanding.

126
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:44,520
In his slogan, syntax is not sufficient for semantics.

127
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:48,500
The system's reply always seemed obviously right to me.

128
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:50,400
Searle is one component.

129
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:55,160
The room as a whole, the rulebook plus the person plus the scratch paper,

130
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,060
is what understands Chinese.

131
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:03,140
Asking whether Searle understands is like asking whether a single neuron understands English.

132
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:05,440
That's the most popular reply.

133
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:09,640
Searle's response was to imagine memorizing the entire rulebook

134
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:16,000
everything in his head. Now he is the whole system, and he still does not understand Chinese,

135
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:21,840
he said. Defenders of the system's reply answer that he would then be implementing a second mind,

136
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:27,300
when he has no introspective access to. Searle rejected that, and every other reply,

137
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:33,440
including connecting the room to a robot body. His positive view was that consciousness is a

138
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:39,940
real biological phenomenon, caused by the specific causal powers of brains, the way digestion is

139
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:46,900
by stomachs. A simulation of digestion digests nothing. A computer simulation of a rainstorm

140
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:52,980
leaves everyone dry. Functionalists have a reply to that last line, and it's worth carrying forward.

141
00:11:53,240 --> 00:12:00,100
A simulated storm leaves you dry, but a simulated calculator really does calculate. Some things are

142
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:05,160
what they do, and some things are what they're made of. The question is which kind of thing a

143
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:11,240
mind is, and that question will come back in episodes 12 and 13. The most developed contemporary

144
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:16,680
version comes from the neuroscientist Anil Seth, who argues that consciousness may depend on

145
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:22,220
properties living systems have and today's computers lack, such as metabolism and continuous

146
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:28,480
self-production. Episode 13 gives that case its full hearing. The short verdict on machines,

147
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:34,740
unlikely on current paths, more plausible only as AI becomes more brain-like or lifelike.

148
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:40,880
Integrated Information Theory, or IIT, sits uneasily on the map.

149
00:12:41,180 --> 00:12:47,020
It's a scientific theory with radical philosophical commitments, so here's the philosophy, and

150
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:48,800
in episode 11, the science.

151
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:55,020
IIT, developed by Giulio Tononi and championed by Christophe Koch, starts from experience

152
00:12:55,020 --> 00:12:56,220
rather than from the brain.

153
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:00,280
It identifies axioms, properties every experience has.

154
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,760
It exists intrinsically for itself.

155
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:05,920
It's specific, this experience, rather than another.

156
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:09,580
It's unified, not decomposable into independent parts.

157
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:12,800
It's definite, with particular borders and contents.

158
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:16,320
And it's structured, composed of distinctions and relations.

159
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:20,540
Then it asks what a physical system must be like to account for each,

160
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:23,260
and answers in terms of cause-effect power.

161
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:27,620
How much the system's current state constrains its own past and future,

162
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:30,840
as a whole, beyond what its parts do independently.

163
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:39,180
is measured by a quantity called phi. The identity claim is bold, and experience simply is a maximally

164
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:44,660
irreducible cause-effect structure. Three implications follow. First, consciousness is

165
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:51,260
graded and widespread. Simple systems can have a little. Second, an exclusion principle, only local

166
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:56,960
maxima of integration are conscious. That means a collection of people, like a nation, is not

167
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:01,820
conscious, because its members are far more integrated than the whole. And third, the verdict

168
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:07,740
famous in AI debates. What matters is the physical causal structure of the hardware,

169
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:13,220
not the software. A conventional digital computer with its clocked, modular architecture

170
00:14:13,220 --> 00:14:19,260
has negligible integrated information whatever program it runs, even a perfect brain simulation.

171
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:25,820
That's the subtitle of Koch's book, The Feeling of Life Itself. Consciousness is widespread but

172
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:32,660
cannot be computed. So on IIT, a perfect simulation of my brain on a GPU cluster is

173
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:39,020
unconscious, but a large enough grid of simple logic gates might be more conscious than I am.

174
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:44,640
Yes, the computer scientist Scott Aronson raised examples like that as a reductio,

175
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:50,640
and Tononi accepted that a suitable grid could be conscious. IIT's verdict on machines,

176
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:56,360
not conventional digital computers, whatever their behavior. Hardware built with the right

177
00:14:56,360 --> 00:15:01,120
physical feedback structure, such as some neuromorphic designs, could in principle be

178
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:10,060
different. Then Type F, Russellian monism and panpsychism. In 1927, Bertrand Russell argued,

179
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:16,240
and Arthur Eddington soon agreed, that physics tells us only about structure. How things relate

180
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:21,800
and behave mathematically, never what they are intrinsically. Mass and charge are defined by

181
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:27,720
what they do, which leaves open what it is that does the doing. The Russellian monist proposal

182
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:34,380
is that the intrinsic nature is experiential or proto-experiential, and that's how consciousness

183
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:39,880
fits into the physical world without breaking physics. Panpsychism follows if you think those

184
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:47,840
intrinsic natures are themselves tiny experiences. In 2006, in a journal symposium that drew a long

185
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:54,980
list of replies, Galen Strawson argued that real physicalism entails panpsychism, since experience

186
00:15:54,980 --> 00:16:01,520
cannot emerge from wholly non-experiential stuff. Philip Goff's Galileo's Error argues that modern

187
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:06,920
science began by deliberately setting sensory qualities aside to make nature mathematical,

188
00:16:07,480 --> 00:16:10,800
And that consciousness is the bill for that decision coming due.

189
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:13,740
The great problem is the combination problem.

190
00:16:14,180 --> 00:16:16,480
William James stated it in 1890.

191
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:23,600
Take a sentence of 12 words, give one word to each of 12 men, and no one will be aware of the whole sentence.

192
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:28,740
How do micro-experiences combine into one unified subject like you?

193
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:35,740
Chalmers has distinguished several versions of the problem, the hardest being how subjects could sum into a new subject.

194
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:39,300
Transpsychists respond with cosmopsychism.

195
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:45,220
The universe as a whole is the fundamental subject, and individual minds are aspects of it.

196
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:49,160
The physicist Sean Carroll gave the sharpest scientific objection

197
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:55,180
in a long conversation with Goff on his podcast Mindscape and later in a 2021 paper.

198
00:16:55,780 --> 00:16:59,620
The laws governing everyday matter, what Carroll calls the core theory,

199
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,060
are tested to extraordinary precision.

200
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:05,560
If consciousness made any difference to what particles do,

201
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:11,440
wrong somewhere we'd have noticed. And if it makes no difference, it's hard to see how it

202
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:17,140
could explain why we talk about consciousness. Goff's reply is that panpsychism does not change

203
00:17:17,140 --> 00:17:22,040
the equations at all. It's a claim about the intrinsic nature of whatever the equations

204
00:17:22,040 --> 00:17:27,540
describe. Carroll's rejoinder is that a nature that adds nothing to the dynamics does no

205
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:34,080
explanatory work. Each side thinks the other has changed the subject. If electrons have tiny

206
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:40,840
experiences? Does my GPU get a bigger one? Not automatically, and that's often misunderstood.

207
00:17:41,260 --> 00:17:47,240
Most panpsychists think a rock is a mere aggregate of micro-experiences without being a subject

208
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:53,320
itself. Whether a computer is more like a brain or more like a rock depends on the combination

209
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:59,220
principle, which nobody has. So panpsychism's verdict on machines is genuinely open.

210
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:02,420
Four more positions, more briefly.

211
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:08,400
Idealism says experience is all there is, and matter is how mind appears from outside.

212
00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:14,100
Bernardo Kastrup's Analytic Idealism proposes a single universal consciousness

213
00:18:14,100 --> 00:18:20,040
that dissociates into individual minds, by analogy with dissociative identity disorder,

214
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:23,560
where one person can present several distinct identities.

215
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:29,280
On that view, the question about a machine is whether it is a separate dissociated mind of its own,

216
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:32,840
and nothing in the theory suggests that a computer would be.

217
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,480
Dualism comes in two main forms.

218
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:42,000
Interactionist dualism, Descartes' view, holds that a non-physical mind pushes the body around.

219
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:48,880
It faces the problem Princess Elizabeth of Bohemia raised in letters to Descartes in 1643.

220
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:51,920
How could something with no extension move matter?

221
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:55,360
adds the problem of causal closure.

222
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:58,760
Naturalistic property dualism, Chalmers' view,

223
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:01,500
adds experience as a fundamental feature

224
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:05,380
tied to physical organization by laws we have not yet found.

225
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:08,020
Remember the fading qualia argument.

226
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:10,920
Chalmers thinks those laws link experience

227
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:12,380
to functional organization,

228
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:14,660
so his dualism, surprisingly,

229
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:16,840
says yes to machine consciousness.

230
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:19,480
The neighboring view, epiphenomenalism,

231
00:19:19,720 --> 00:19:21,720
makes experience causally idle,

232
00:19:23,560 --> 00:19:28,760
puzzle, the meta-problem again. If experience does nothing, why do we keep talking about it?

233
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:35,980
Mysterianism, from Colin McGinn in 1989, holds that there is a natural answer, but that human

234
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:42,480
minds are cognitively close to it, the way a dog is close to calculus. A neighboring stance says

235
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:48,220
phenomenal consciousness lies outside the reach of science altogether. The verdict on machines

236
00:19:48,220 --> 00:19:49,640
is that we may never know.

237
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:51,080
And the quantum route.

238
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:54,620
Roger Penrose argued in The Emperor's New Mind

239
00:19:54,620 --> 00:19:56,080
and Shadows of the Mind

240
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:58,060
that human mathematical understanding

241
00:19:58,060 --> 00:19:59,780
is not algorithmic,

242
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:02,300
using Gödel's incompleteness theorems.

243
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:05,000
We can supposedly see the truth of statements

244
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,940
that any given formal system cannot prove.

245
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:10,620
Everyone I know in machine learning

246
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:12,760
thinks the Gödel argument is wrong,

247
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:15,200
but they usually cannot say why.

248
00:20:15,540 --> 00:20:16,620
Here's the why.

249
00:20:18,220 --> 00:20:23,720
know that our own reasoning is consistent, and we do not. Most logicians reject it on those grounds,

250
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:28,960
but Penrose paired it with a physical proposal. Wave function collapse caused by gravity,

251
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:33,680
which he calls objective reduction. With the anesthesiologist Stuart Hameroff,

252
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:37,980
he proposed that orchestrated versions of this collapse happen in microtubules,

253
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:44,800
the protein scaffolding inside neurons. Hence Orch-OR. The main objection is decoherence.

254
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:59,040
The brain is warm and wet, and in 2000, the physicist Max Tegmark calculated that quantum coherence there should vanish in far less than a trillionth of a second, while neurons work on timescales of thousandths of a second.

255
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:02,140
Hameroff and colleagues disputed the calculation.

256
00:21:02,440 --> 00:21:04,480
Then the physics met an experiment.

257
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:14,980
In 2020, a team deep underground at Gran Sasso in Italy looked for the faint radiation that the simplest gravity-related collapse model predicts, and found none.

258
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:24,400
A 2022 follow-up argued that under that simplest model, Orch-OR, becomes highly implausible, though more flexible versions survive.

259
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:30,900
On the biological side, a 2024 study found that rats given a microtubule-stabilizing drug

260
00:21:30,900 --> 00:21:34,580
took about a minute longer to lose their writing reflex under anesthesia.

261
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:39,280
Suggestive, but only eight treated rats, no quantum effects measured,

262
00:21:39,480 --> 00:21:41,540
and an anesthetic with many other targets.

263
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:49,000
In 2025, the study's senior author published a review arguing that the experimental case is now strong.

264
00:21:49,340 --> 00:21:52,080
Most neuroscientists remain unconvinced.

265
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:57,560
There are more cautious quantum proposals. Chalmers and the philosopher Kelvin McQueen

266
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:03,220
have explored whether consciousness could cause collapse, combining IIT with a mathematical

267
00:22:03,220 --> 00:22:08,460
collapse model, and argued that versions not already ruled out could in principle be tested

268
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:14,980
on quantum computers. And Tegmark himself proposed, in 2014, treating consciousness as a state of

269
00:22:14,980 --> 00:22:20,740
matter, which he nicknamed perceptronium, defined by how it processes information rather than by

270
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:28,800
electrons. Orch-O-R, S-verdict on machines, is the clearest. Classical computers cannot be

271
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:33,140
conscious, because consciousness requires a physical process they do not perform.

272
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:40,580
Finally, a position about positions. Eric S. H. Witzgabel calls it crazyism. Every theory of

273
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:45,600
consciousness, he argues, has at least one implication that sounds bizarre. Functionalism

274
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:51,020
makes the China brain conscious, IIT certain logic gate grids, panpsychism electrons.

275
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:56,780
Illusionism denies what seems undeniable, and dualism adds a new ingredient to physics.

276
00:22:57,180 --> 00:23:01,000
Something crazy must be true, we just do not know which crazy thing.

277
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:05,480
His paper, If Materialism is True, the United States is Probably Conscious,

278
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:08,600
makes the point with the China brain's real-world cousin.

279
00:23:08,860 --> 00:23:12,680
By criteria liberal enough to include rabbits and intelligent aliens,

280
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:19,120
the United States, a richly integrated, self-monitoring, goal-pursuing system, would seem to qualify.

281
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:22,960
IIT disagrees, thanks to its exclusion principle.

282
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:27,800
Most readers find the conclusion unbelievable, which is precisely his point.

283
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:32,460
Give me the scoreboard, which positions let machines in.

284
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:37,480
Functionalism, yes, with the right organization and the fight is over grain.

285
00:23:38,820 --> 00:23:43,580
Representationalism, possibly if the machine's representations are genuinely about the world,

286
00:23:43,740 --> 00:23:45,540
which may depend on its training history.

287
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:52,340
illusionism yes if it has the right self-model for the right reasons chalmers's naturalistic

288
00:23:52,340 --> 00:23:59,160
dualism yes by organizational invariance an activism and biological naturalism unlikely

289
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:06,280
without something like life iit not on conventional digital hardware whatever it does panpsychism

290
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:13,740
undetermined pending a combination principle castrop's idealism probably not orch o r not

291
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:17,300
Mysterianism, we may never know.

292
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:22,320
And the octopus, most positions on the map let it in, which is worth noticing.

293
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:25,980
The disagreements cluster at machines, not at animals.

294
00:24:26,460 --> 00:24:27,360
Two more things.

295
00:24:27,660 --> 00:24:31,740
The positions that say yes do not all say yes to the same systems.

296
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:36,500
A functionalist and an illusionist could disagree about a given language model.

297
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:41,260
And the dividing line is not between scientific and unscientific views.

298
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:44,400
It runs through the middle of the scientific community.

299
00:24:45,540 --> 00:24:51,440
Before we leave the map, a short detour clearly marked. The edges. These are ideas that are

300
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:57,240
popular, sometimes striking, and often recommended in books and podcasts, but that sit outside

301
00:24:57,240 --> 00:25:02,800
mainstream science or have little supporting evidence so far. For each, I'll give the claim

302
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:08,660
and its status, without mockery and without endorsement. Federico Fagin led the design of

303
00:25:08,660 --> 00:25:14,820
the first commercial microprocessor. In his 2024 book Irreducible, and with the physicist

304
00:25:15,540 --> 00:25:21,340
Moro Dariano, he argues that consciousness and free will are fundamental, and that experience

305
00:25:21,340 --> 00:25:27,840
belongs to quantum systems in pure states, whose information, like experience, is private and

306
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:34,180
cannot be copied. So classical computers can never be conscious. Status, a theoretical proposal.

307
00:25:34,520 --> 00:25:40,040
Its authors have sketched possible experiments, but none has been carried out. From the Qualia

308
00:25:40,040 --> 00:25:46,020
Research Institute comes the symmetry theory of valence, proposed by Michael Johnson in Principia

309
00:25:46,020 --> 00:25:52,540
Qualia in 2016. How good or bad an experience feels is encoded in the symmetry of the mathematical

310
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:59,120
object that describes it, with harmony as pleasure and dissonance as suffering. Its proponents have

311
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:05,560
proposed brain imaging tests based on the harmonics of neural activity. Status? A conjecture without

312
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:12,140
independent confirmation. Nearby is David Pierce's non-materialist physicalism, which holds that the

313
00:26:12,140 --> 00:26:17,280
intrinsic nature of the physical is experiential, and that the unity of experience comes from

314
00:26:17,280 --> 00:26:23,680
quantum coherence in neurons over extraordinarily short timescales. Pierce proposes a test that

315
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:30,860
current technology cannot yet perform. Status. Speculative and untested. The cognitive scientist

316
00:26:30,860 --> 00:26:36,260
Donald Hoffman argues in the case against reality that evolution tunes perception to fitness

317
00:26:36,260 --> 00:26:41,360
rather than truth, backed by published simulations that others continue to debate.

318
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:46,860
Beyond that, he proposes that reality is fundamentally a network of conscious agents.

319
00:26:47,420 --> 00:26:53,260
Status. The simulations are published science. The conscious agent theory has no confirmed

320
00:26:53,260 --> 00:26:58,840
predictions yet. Thomas Campbell, a physicist, argues in his self-published trilogy,

321
00:26:58,840 --> 00:27:04,380
my big toe that physical reality is a virtual reality within a larger consciousness system.

322
00:27:04,640 --> 00:27:11,020
He has proposed variations of the double-slit experiment as tests, and in 2024, a non-profit

323
00:27:11,020 --> 00:27:17,360
he founded announced that researchers had begun the first of them. Status. No peer-reviewed results

324
00:27:17,360 --> 00:27:24,040
that I could find. Itzhak Bentov's 1977 book, Stalking the Wild Pendulum, describes consciousness

325
00:27:24,040 --> 00:27:30,580
in terms of vibration and oscillation underlying matter. Status, a popular, speculative book that

326
00:27:30,580 --> 00:27:35,780
was never developed into a testable theory. Ken Wilber's Integral Theory maps consciousness,

327
00:27:36,160 --> 00:27:41,720
culture, and contemplative development into quadrants and stages. Status, a philosophical

328
00:27:41,720 --> 00:27:48,320
and spiritual synthesis, not an empirical theory. Two historical entries. The physicist Wolfgang

329
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:53,960
Pauly and the psychiatrist Carl Jung, over decades of correspondence, explored the idea that

330
00:27:54,040 --> 00:27:59,640
mind and matter are two aspects of a single underlying reality, along with Jung's notion

331
00:27:59,640 --> 00:28:06,280
of synchronicity. Status, a serious subject for historians and philosophers, not an empirical

332
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:12,980
theory. And Julian Jaynes' 1976 book On the Bicameral Mind argues that introspective consciousness

333
00:28:12,980 --> 00:28:19,720
arose only about 3,000 years ago, and that earlier people experienced one hemisphere's commands as

334
00:28:19,720 --> 00:28:27,200
the voices of gods. Status, admired as a provocation. Its historical claims are widely disputed,

335
00:28:27,360 --> 00:28:32,920
though its emphasis on language and shaping introspection has defenders. What these ideas

336
00:28:32,920 --> 00:28:37,660
share is ambition, and the test for each is the same one we apply to the mainstream,

337
00:28:38,040 --> 00:28:42,260
a prediction that someone could actually check. That ends the detour.

338
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:48,240
So in the next two episodes, we turn to what can be checked. First, the evidence,

339
00:28:49,720 --> 00:28:54,900
experiments, altered states, animals, and the remarkable tools that can now detect consciousness

340
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:59,580
in people who cannot move. Then the theories put on trial against each other.

